WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.771
Metadata you will want later.

2
00:00:02.051 --> 00:00:06.381
Every document, and every chunk cut from it, should carry a metadata record.

3
00:00:06.661 --> 00:00:12.059
Capturing it now is cheap; reconstructing it after a million chunks are indexed is not.

4
00:00:12.339 --> 00:00:15.347
A record that pays for itself: Source identity.

5
00:00:15.627 --> 00:00:21.685
A stable id, the original location (path, URL, or system record id), and the title.

6
00:00:21.965 --> 00:00:23.843
Week 4’s citations link here.

7
00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:25.701
Location within the source.

8
00:00:25.981 --> 00:00:32.488
Page number for PDFs, heading path for structured documents, and character offsets into the cleaned text.

9
00:00:32.768 --> 00:00:44.651
Claude’s citations feature, for example, reports page ranges for PDFs and character ranges for plain text, so offsets let you map a citation back to the exact place in the original.

10
00:00:44.931 --> 00:00:46.019
Time and version.

11
00:00:46.299 --> 00:00:49.264
Document date or version, plus when you ingested it.

12
00:00:49.544 --> 00:00:54.984
Retrieval can then prefer the current policy over last year’s, and you can find stale content.

13
00:00:55.264 --> 00:00:56.181
Permissions.

14
00:00:56.461 --> 00:01:00.813
The groups or users allowed to read the source, copied from the source system.

15
00:01:01.093 --> 00:01:04.293
Lesson 6 makes the store filter on this before ranking.

16
00:01:04.573 --> 00:01:11.251
If a document has no permission record, fail closed: make it visible to nobody until someone fixes the record.

17
00:01:11.531 --> 00:01:12.448
Provenance.

18
00:01:12.728 --> 00:01:16.376
A content hash, the parser and its version, and the strategy used.

19
00:01:16.656 --> 00:01:20.624
When you upgrade a parser, you can find exactly which chunks to rebuild.

20
00:01:20.904 --> 00:01:26.003
A useful test: for every field, name the query, filter, or audit that will use it.

21
00:01:26.283 --> 00:01:31.872
Fields nobody uses are clutter; filters without fields are bugs waiting for a deadline.
